* sg13g2-pll :: sim/sg13cmos5l-lock-detector-window (issue #52, Part of #16)
* Recovery + supply-current sub-deck: the base 3-copy block
* (XR/XIL/XIU) split out of ../testbench/tb_lock_ladder.sp.tmpl's own
* skeleton, on its own so it can be simulated separately from the
* phase-error ladder points (../testbench/tb_lock_ladder_point.sp.tmpl).
*
* WHY THIS SPLIT EXISTS (issue #52).  Issue #38's RECORD-001 found the
* integrating node's R*C time constant 23-1412x SHORTER than one reference
* period at every corner -- issue #52's fix makes R*C many multiples of the
* SLOWEST reference period instead (spec/porting-plan.md row 2, amended by
* DR-005 to ~3.5-24.4 MHz). That inherently means the block's own settling
* now takes many reference cycles (by design -- that is what makes it an
* integrator instead of a combinational pass-through), so a corner's own
* full-ladder settling transient can no longer complete in a handful of
* reference periods the way ../records/RECORD-001's original tstop=4*tref
* did. Measured directly, at mos_tt/res_typ/27C/3.3V/3.5 MHz with the resized
* block and a 54-reference-cycle tstop: the merged tb_lock_ladder.sp.tmpl
* deck (21 copies -- 9 ladder points x 2 start states + XR/XIL/XIU) did NOT
* complete in 400 s, while the same corner run as 1 recovery deck + 9
* separate 2-copy ladder-point decks completed in about 200 s total. The
* per-timestep cost does not scale linearly with copy count on this deck:
* every copy's own independently-timed pulse edges are new ngspice
* breakpoints shared by the WHOLE transient's adaptive step control, so more
* simultaneous copies force smaller globally-accepted steps everywhere, not
* just proportionally more work.
* Splitting one big deck into many small, independent ngspice invocations
* (this file, plus one ../testbench/tb_lock_ladder_point.sp.tmpl invocation
* per ladder point) turns that into an embarrassingly-parallel, tractable
* sweep -- see ../testbench/run.sh's own "per-corner N_cycles" note for the
* settling-duration side of the same fix.
*
* Reports exactly the same trec/lr_*/vr_*/idd_* .meas names
* ../testbench/tb_lock_ladder.sp.tmpl's own base section did, so
* ../testbench/gen_ladder.py's `reduce` subcommand reads this file's output
* exactly the way it always read the base section of the merged deck --
* run.sh concatenates this deck's stdout with every per-point deck's stdout
* before handing the combined text to `reduce`.
*
* Placeholders (substituted by ../testbench/run.sh, not meant to be run
* directly): @CORNER_MOS@, @CORNER_RES@, @TEMP@, @VSUP@, @VMID@, @TREF@,
* @TRST@, @TAUBIG@ (the deepest ladder point's own tau, for XIU's
* out-of-window supply-current copy), @TSTEP@, @TSTOP@, @TSETTLE@, @DUT@.

.lib @PDK_ROOT@/@PDK@/libs.tech/ngspice/models/cornerMOShv.lib @CORNER_MOS@
.lib @PDK_ROOT@/@PDK@/libs.tech/ngspice/models/cornerRES.lib @CORNER_RES@
.include @PDK_ROOT@/@PDK@/libs.tech/ngspice/models/cap_cmomi.lib
.option scale=1 temp=@TEMP@
.global sub!

.include @DUT@

.param vsup=@VSUP@
.param tref=@TREF@
.param trst=@TRST@
.param ttr=100p
.param ttd='tref'

Vsub sub! 0 0
Vdd vdd 0 dc @VSUP@

* ---- zero-phase-error stimulus (UP and DN identical: XOR sees nothing) ----
Vup0 up0 0 pulse(0 @VSUP@ 'ttd' 'ttr' 'ttr' 'trst' 'tref')
Vdn0 dn0 0 pulse(0 @VSUP@ 'ttd' 'ttr' 'ttr' 'trst' 'tref')

* ---- recovery copy: starts fully discharged with no phase error ----------
Xr up0 dn0 lkr vdd 0 lock_detector

* ---- per-block supply current, on their own ideal rails ------------------
* XIL: in-window steady state (tau = 0), the current the block draws while
*      the PLL is locked.  XIU: out-of-window (tau = @TAUBIG@), the current it
*      draws while the flag is being exercised every reference cycle.
Vddl vddl 0 dc @VSUP@
Vddu vddu 0 dc @VSUP@
Xil up0 dn0 lkil vddl 0 lock_detector
Vupu upu 0 pulse(0 @VSUP@ 'ttd' 'ttr' 'ttr' '@TAUBIG@+trst' 'tref')
Vdnu dnu 0 pulse(0 @VSUP@ 'ttd+@TAUBIG@' 'ttr' 'ttr' 'trst' 'tref')
Xiu upu dnu lkiu vddu 0 lock_detector

.ic v(xr.vwin)=0 v(xil.vwin)=@VSUP@ v(xiu.vwin)=0

* SOLVER-EFFORT NOTE -- itl4=5000 gmin=1e-11 (issue #66, Part of #16).
* Neither is an accuracy relaxation: reltol/abstol/vntol/chgtol are untouched,
* and both are inert where the default already converged (numbers below).
* Both are needed -- neither alone clears every abort measured.
*
* WHY.  Issue #66 makes XMPD a weak, long-channel device, so the integrating
* node now SITS at intermediate voltages for the whole run instead of railing.
* That holds schmitt_hv's input in its own high-gain region and keeps the
* coincidence gate's nodes near their switching points for tens of microseconds
* of simulated time, which is where ngspice's defaults give out.  Three
* measured hard aborts, each an ngspice exit 1 and therefore a whole-campaign
* abort under run.sh's run_ngspice_or_die:
*   (1) mos_ff/res_wcs/-40C, 24.4 MHz, tau = 1.4 x twin_r:
*       "Timestep too small; ... trouble with node xa.xxor.n1"
*   (2) mos_ff/res_wcs/-40C, 24.4 MHz, tau = 1.6 x twin_r: same, node xa.wide
*   (3) mos_tt/res_wcs/27C, 3.5 MHz, tau = 2.0 x twin_r: same, node
*       xa5.xxor.xn2.mid1
*
* ITL4 raises the Newton-iteration budget before the step is cut.  GMIN is
* raised 10x above ngspice's 1e-12 default because (3)'s node is nand2_hv's own
* SERIES-STACK INTERNAL NODE `mid1`, which has no DC path to any rail whenever
* both stacked NMOS are off -- a floating node is exactly what gmin exists for,
* and 1e-11 S is a 100 GOhm shunt, i.e. ~3e4 x weaker than XRPU's own 1.35-3.30
* MOhm pull-up on the one high-impedance node this block actually cares about.
*
* Measured impact (same decks, same points):
*   - ITL4 10 -> 500 at already-converging points: 2.41949 -> 2.41919 V
*     (0.012%) and 1.46963 -> 1.46963 V (0.000%).
*   - ITL4 500 -> 5000 at abort (1): 1.84852 -> 1.84980 V (0.07%).
*   - adding gmin=1e-11 on top of itl4=5000: 1.84980 -> 1.84980 V, 0.444269 ->
*     0.444269 V, 3.15340 -> 3.15340 V.  Zero change to six digits at every
*     point checked.
* Coverage: itl4=500 clears (1) only; itl4=5000 clears (1) and (2); itl4=50000
* still does not clear (3); gmin=1e-11 alone clears (2) and (3) but not (1).
* Measured and REJECTED: method=gear clears (1) but moves the answer materially
* (1.849 -> 1.733 V) and does not clear (2); gmin=1e-9 and gmin=1e-15 clear
* nothing extra; reltol=5e-4 clears nothing extra; trtol=1 clears (2) and (3)
* but is a truncation-error relaxation, i.e. an accuracy knob, so it was not
* used.
.options reltol=1e-3 abstol=1e-10 vntol=1e-5 chgtol=1e-13 itl4=5000 gmin=1e-11
.tran @TSTEP@ @TSTOP@

.control
save lkr xr.vwin vddl#branch vddu#branch
run
meas tran trec when v(xr.vwin)=@VMID@ rise=1
* The recovery copy's own settled LOCK level defines which rail this block
* drives in the in-window state.  The reduction reads the polarity from here
* instead of assuming it -- see ../records/RECORD-001 Finding 1.
meas tran lr_min min v(lkr) from=@TSETTLE@ to=@TSTOP@
meas tran lr_max max v(lkr) from=@TSETTLE@ to=@TSTOP@
meas tran lr_avg avg v(lkr) from=@TSETTLE@ to=@TSTOP@
meas tran vr_min min v(xr.vwin) from=@TSETTLE@ to=@TSTOP@
meas tran vr_max max v(xr.vwin) from=@TSETTLE@ to=@TSTOP@
meas tran idd_lock   avg i(vddl) from=@TSETTLE@ to=@TSTOP@
meas tran idd_unlock avg i(vddu) from=@TSETTLE@ to=@TSTOP@
.endc
.end
